<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jalore</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jalor, Jabalipura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jalore, or Jalor, is a town in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, below a granite hill crowned by one of the strongest forts of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>. Taken from the Paramaras around 1160 by the Chahamana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipala">Kirtipala</a>, it was the capital of the Songara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> captured it in 1311. It later belonged to Afghan chiefs and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores of Marwar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalore.jpg" alt="Jalore" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Surya Gahlot · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kailash_Dham_Bishangarh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jalore, also called Jalor and known as the Granite City, is in the western Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and is the administrative headquarters of Jalore District. It lies south of the Sukri River, a tributary of the Luni, and the Jawai River passes through it. The town is about 140 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and 489 km from the state capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>. The hill on which its fort stands was known as Suvarngiri or Songir, the Golden Mount.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the town was called Jabalipura, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> saint Jabali. It was flourishing in the 8th century, and according to some historical sources one branch of the Pratihara empire ruled there in the 8th and 9th centuries. The writer Uddyotana-suri composed the Kuvalaya-mala at Jabalipura in 779 CE, in the reign of the Gurjara-Pratihara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a>. After the Paramara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja">Vakpati Munja</a> invaded the region in the late 10th century, he divided the conquered lands among his princes, and Jalore was given to Dharnivarah Paramara. The town was ruled by the Paramaras in the 10th century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipala">Kirtipala</a>, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhana">Alhana</a>, the Chahamana ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadol">Nadol</a>, captured Jalore from the Paramaras and founded the Jalore line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a>. His son Samarasimha succeeded him, followed by Udayasimha, who expanded the kingdom by recapturing Nadol and Mandor from the Turks and, during his reign, was a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Samantasimha and then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadadeva">Kanhadadeva</a> followed. In 1311 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, attacked and captured Jalore, and Kanhadadeva and his son Viramadeva died defending it.</p><p>Later the Turkic rulers of Palanpur State in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> briefly ruled Jalor in the 16th century, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. It was restored to Marwar in 1704 and remained part of that kingdom until shortly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jalore was the hometown of Jaiwanta Bai, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>, who was the daughter of Akhey Raj Songara. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> rulers of Ratlam used the Jalore fort to keep their treasure. The town has 12 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monasteries and 13 mosques. It is known as the home of the Marwari horse, an indigenous breed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jalore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Several Pratihara and Gurjara lineages existed, including the Pratiharas of Mandavyapura near Jodhpur and the Gurjaras of Nandipuri in Gujarat, but the imperial line begins with Nagabhata I, whose base is variously placed at Ujjain or in the <b>Jalor</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Jalor</b> branch of the Chahamanas (Chauhans) descended from the Chahamanas of Naddula.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Chahamanas of Jalor</a></li><li><blockquote>A branch founded by Kirtipala, son of Alhana, moved to <b>Jalor</b>, where the Songara Chauhans ruled until Alauddin Khalji took the fort in 1311.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-naddula">Chahamanas of Naddula</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling Lohani family, of Afghan origin, served the sultans of the region and by tradition held <b>Jalore</b> before establishing themselves at Palanpur.</blockquote><a href="/state/palanpur-state">Palanpur State</a></li></ul></section>
